      "justification": "Unlike the defender split, the midfield split has real content on both sides. This cluster of 94 midfielders sits below the midfield mean on every attacking measure, with shots on target per 90 0.69 standard deviations below it, non-penalty expected goals per 90 0.67 standard deviations below, shots per 90 0.66 standard deviations below and expected assists per 90 0.62 standard deviations below, and it is the only cluster in the study whose positive deviations are all defensive or disciplinary rather than attacking: interceptions per 90 sit 0.55 standard deviations above the midfield mean and tackles won per 90 0.45 standard deviations above it. The negative attacking side of the contrast is the larger part of it, which is why the name records the ball winning tilt second rather than first. The members closest to the centroid are Sam Morsy, Kobbie Mainoo, James Garner, Wilfred Ndidi and Lucas Bergvall. This is the best supported of the three within-group splits: bootstrap resampling gives a mean adjusted Rand index of 0.837 for midfielders with a standard deviation of 0.113 and a minimum of 0.529 over 200 resamples, so the partition survives resampling in a way the defender and forward partitions do not.",
